      "text": "To determine the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\dots, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we first need to understand the structure of the floor function applied to the expression \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\).\n\nFor a given \\(n\\), the expression \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) will yield a number in the range from 0 to \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\leq \\frac{n^2}{1000} < 1\\), with the smallest integer \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) being achieved when \\(n\\) is at its maximum value where the quotient is exactly 1. This happens when \\(n = 31\\) because \\(31^2 = 961\\) and \\(\\frac{961}{1000} = 0.961\\) so \\(\\left\\lfloor 0.961 \\right\\rfloor = 0\\).\n\nLet's examine the values of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 31. For \\(n = 1\\), \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\). For \\(n = 2\\), \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\). For \\(n = 3\\), \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\). This pattern continues until \\(n = 31\\), where \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{31^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 961/1000 = 0.961\\), and \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{32^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\) because \\(32^2 = 1024 > 1000\\). So, \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{31^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1\\) and \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{32^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\).\n\nFrom \\(n = 32\\), the expression \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) starts to decrease continuously. For \\(n = 32\\), \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{32^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1\\). For \\(n = 33\\), \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{33^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\), and so on. This pattern continues until \\(n = 1000\\), where \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1000\\).\n\nTherefore, the distinct values in the list are \\(0, 1\\), and the numbers between these values are the integers from 0 to 999. The number of distinct integers from 0 to 999 is 1000.\n\nSo, the number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{1000}\\).",
